  how "result": "============================================================\nBLANKET CODE GOVERNANCE POLICIES\nThe following policies MUST be followed when generating code.\n============================================================\n\n--- Policy 1/5: No Hardcoded Secrets [🔴 MUST] ---\nPrevents API keys, passwords, tokens, and connection strings from being hardcoded in generated code.\n\nCRITICAL POLICY -- No Hardcoded Secrets:\\n- NEVER include API keys, passwords, tokens, connection strings, or any credentials directly in code.\\n- Always use environment variables (os.environ, process.env) or a secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, etc.).\\n- If an example requires a credential, use a clearly fake placeholder like \"YOUR_API_KEY_HERE\" and add a comment explaining where to store it.\\n- Flag any existing hardcoded secrets you encounter and suggest migration to env vars.\n\n--- Policy 2/5: Require Input Validation [🔴 MUST] ---\nMandates input validation on all external-facing functions, API endpoints, and data entry points.\n\nPOLICY -- Require Input Validation:\\n- All API endpoint parameters MUST be validated before processing (type, range, length, format).\\n- Use schema validation libraries (Pydantic, Zod, Joi) for complex inputs.\\n- Sanitize string inputs to prevent injection attacks (SQL, XSS, command injection).\\n- Return clear 400-level error responses with human-readable messages for invalid input.\\n- Never trust client-side validation alone -- always validate server-side.\n\n--- Policy 3/5: Enforce Error Handling [🟡 SHOULD] ---\nEnsures all generated code includes proper error handling, logging, and graceful failure paths.\n\nPOLICY -- Enforce Error Handling:\\n- Every function that performs I/O (network, file, database) MUST have try/catch or equivalent error handling.\\n- Catch specific exception types, not bare except/catch-all.\\n- Log errors with context (function name, input parameters, timestamp) before re-raising or returning.\\n- Always provide a meaningful error message to the caller.\\n- For async operations, ensure errors propagate correctly through the chain.\n\n--- Policy 4/5: Enforce Naming Conventions [🔵 RECOMMENDED] ---\nEnsures generated code follows consistent naming standards appropriate to the language.\n\nPOLICY -- Enforce Naming Conventions:\\n- Python: snake_case for functions/variables, PascalCase for classes, UPPER_SNAKE for constants.\\n- TypeScript/JavaScript: camelCase for functions/variables, PascalCase for classes/components/types, UPPER_SNAKE for constants.\\n- Java: camelCase for methods/variables, PascalCase for classes, UPPER_SNAKE for constants.\\n- Use descriptive names that convey intent -- no single-letter variables except loop counters.\\n- Boolean variables/functions should read as questions: is_active, has_permission, can_edit.\n\n--- Policy 5/5: Block Deprecated API Usage [🟡 SHOULD] ---\nPrevents generated code from using deprecated libraries, functions, or API patterns.\n\nPOLICY -- Block Deprecated API Usage:\\n- Do NOT use deprecated functions or libraries. Always prefer the modern replacement.\\n- Common deprecations to avoid: datetime.utcnow() -> datetime.now(UTC), pkg_resources -> importlib.resources, urllib2 -> urllib3/requests, componentWillMount -> useEffect.\\n- If a library version is specified in the project context, respect its API surface.\\n- When suggesting dependencies, prefer actively maintained packages with recent releases.\\n- Flag any deprecated patterns you see in existing code context.\n\n============================================================\nEND OF BLANKET POLICIES\n============================================================",

